<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cornwall</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kernow</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cornwall is a county in the far south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, a peninsula bounded by the Atlantic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a>. It was home to the British kingdom of Dumnonia and its Cornish-speaking successors, and was absorbed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> by the tenth century, keeping its Celtic language into the eighteenth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">Tin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mining were central to its economy for millennia, and its mining landscape is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cornwall.jpg" alt="Cornwall" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Werner Wilmes · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Michael%27s_Mount_View.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
